 "Ji Ling", "context": "Ji Ling's entire military career and significance are framed by the period of political fragmentation and warlordism during the late Eastern Han dynasty."}], "places": [{"name": "Xiaopei, China", "role": "Major battle site"}, {"name": "Shouchun, China", "role": "Warlord base"}, {"name": "Yang Province, China", "role": "Area of campaigns"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ji Ling", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ji-ling"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1323201", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ji_Ling"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Ji Ling, as a key general for Yuan Shu, played a direct role in the military conflicts that destabilized the Eastern Han dynasty, contributing to the conditions that led to the Three Kingdoms period and shaping the early interactions between major figures like Liu Bei and Lü Bu.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-13T14:10:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29255015793", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Ji Ling was a military general serving under the warlord Yuan Shu during the late Eastern Han dynasty of China.", "newValue": "Ji Ling was a formidable military general who served under the ambitious warlord Yuan Shu during the tumultuous late Eastern Han dynasty of China (c. 189–220 CE). Operating primarily within the fertile plains of central China, his significance stems from his direct involvement in the internecine conflicts that ultimately fragmented the empire, particularly his engagements against key figures like Liu Bei and Lü Bu. His career encapsulates the brutal realities of a collapsing imperial order, where personal loyalty and military prowess determined the fleeting fortunes of regional power blocs.

Ji Ling's most notable military actions centered around Yuan Shu's campaigns to expand his territory and influence. In 196 CE, he commanded Yuan Shu's forces in a major offensive against Liu Bei, besieging Xiaopei (modern-day Pei County, Jiangsu). This siege became famous for the intervention of the warlord Lü Bu, who, wishing to maintain a balance of power, dramatically mediated the conflict through his 'archery feat at the gate,' where he shot an arrow through a halberd from a hundred paces, compelling both sides to temporarily withdraw. Despite this setback, Ji Ling remained a trusted commander, later participating in Yuan Shu's ill-fated declaration of a new Zhong dynasty in 197 CE, a move that alienated many and accelerated Yuan Shu's decline.

His military contributions, though ultimately unsuccessful in securing Yuan Shu's long-term ambitions, highlight the strategic importance of commanders in an era defined by martial strength. Ji Ling's defeat and death around 220 CE, likely during Yuan Shu's final collapse or shortly thereafter, marked the end of a career inextricably linked to one of the period's most prominent, yet ultimately failed, warlords. His actions, particularly against Liu Bei, indirectly contributed to the complex web of alliances and antagonisms that would eventually coalesce into the Three Kingdoms period (220–280 CE)."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-13T14:10:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29255015793", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "101 CE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "101 CE"}], "newValue": ["The collapse of centralized authority within the Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum across China.", "The emergence of powerful regional warlords, such as Yuan Shu, led to widespread military conflict and competition for resources.", "Intense rivalries among warlords like Yuan Shu, Liu Bei, and Lü Bu necessitated strong military commanders to lead their armies."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-13T14:10:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29255015793", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "220 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "220 CE"}], "newValue": ["Ji Ling's military campaigns contributed to the prolonged instability and warfare that characterized the late Eastern Han period.", "His defeat in various engagements, particularly the siege of Xiaopei, weakened Yua